it's the first week of june and you know  what that means it's time for another dr  sather channel update so in this one i'm  going to talk about what we did during  the month of may which was very  important and busy month  and what we've got lined up for the  month of june a month in which i'll  actually get a little bit of vacation  but where we've got a lot of other  interesting stuff lined up as well  so we passed an important milestone in  may and i shot a video about that so i'm  not gonna i'm not gonna go into any  depth about it i've got the link uh in  the description for this video  and that milestone was a million views  so for a philosophy focused channel  that's kind of a big thing you know if  we were doing  uh little kitty cats or animal attacks  or  you know  who knows what else uh people getting  hit in the groin that would not be a  very big  very large number of views but for  philosophy videos some of which are you  know an hour an hour and a half long  that's kind of a a big thing  so  i talked in that that video um about  some of the things we're going to roll  out this fall  none of those are happening in june so  i'm going to kind of skip over those  here  um what else happened in in may well  marist classes came to a close the  face-to-face classes  and not long after that  my online class with marist began i  teach an online 10-week ethics class in  the summer and i often teach a four-week  ethics class in the  winter session  although i don't know whether that's  going to happen this time around  and i've already got you know 31  students enrolled  we just finished our first week  and it's it's going you know fairly well  i had to actually send out the the email  notices to a bunch of students saying  hey you know online doesn't mean  correspondence you gotta gotta  participate a bit more  we did two glimpses into existence  existentialism series lectures at the  kingston public library this month in  part because the weird scheduling in  april how easter fell and all the other  things that we had going on  so  we did a nietzsche one at the beginning  and arika won just this last weekend  we're getting more people coming to them  they're starting to really get some some  traction in the local community in the  hudson valley and it's a lot of fun some  of the same people are showing up  lecture after lecture and and there's  some continuity to the discussions as a  result  we did a philosophy forum with rob skiff  from aplerno which is a for-profit  educational company which is uh really  putting adjuncts in the driver's seat  not only for  developing class content but for paying  a living wage i did the breakdown on how  much  the  college classes that i'm teaching online  not for a player know  cost the students and how much of that i  actually receive  and it was less than ten percent of what  they they charge those students i  actually get paid so all the rest is  going to overhead in one form or or  another  with the player know i get 80 percent  which is really extraordinary so  we can afford to charge less much much  less for the class  and have smaller classes and still  make a better  living because we don't have to pay all  these administrators and staff and this  and that  and you know build build beautiful  buildings for the college it it's a way  to actually make things center once  again around the teacher and the student  the way it ought to be in education  so that was a really cool discussion  that we had we talked about student  loans we talked about the plight of  adjuncts we talked about where education  is going we talked about online  platforms  covered a lot of ground  i did another theology matters with the  pelus that's a radio show that  i've been contributing to  showing up as a guest talking about  various  christian philosophers and sometimes the  the atheist bad boy philosophers too we  did one on david hume back in the fall  this time it was on saint augustine and  his 